==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|-}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|malice}}, from {{der|en|la|malitia|t=badness, bad quality, ill-will, spite}}, from {{m|la|malus|t=bad}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|măl'ĭs}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈmælɪs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-malice.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# Intention to harm or deprive in an illegal or immoral way. Desire to take pleasure in another's misfortune.
#* '''1981''', [[w:Philip K. Dick|Philip K. Dick]], ''Valis'', {{ISBN|0-553-20594-3}}, page 67:
#*: {{...}} not only was there no gratitude (which he could psychologically handle) but downright '''malice''' showed itself instead.
# {{lbl|en|law}} An intention to do [[injury]] to another [[party]], which in many [[jurisdiction]]s is a distinguishing factor between the [[crime]]s of [[murder]] and [[manslaughter]].
{{slim-wikipedia|Malice (law)}}

